"Fibonacci enumeration conjecture for simple permutations sortable by two parallel pop stacks | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/367479/fibonacci-enumeration-conjecture-for-simple-permutations-sor)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Fibonacci enumeration conjecture for simple permutations sortable by two parallel pop stacks ... Lapo Cioni, Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith, “Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass”, arXiv:2503.08285 (2025).\n\nenumerative-combinatorics permutation-patterns sorting-algorithms\n\n# Fibonacci enumeration conjecture for simple permutations sortable by two parallel pop stacks\n\nAbout 1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nFor each $n\\geq 0$n≥0, let $a_n$a n​ be the number of simple permutations of size $n$n that can be sorted by a machine consisting of two pop stacks in parallel, with entries allowed to bypass the pop stacks. Let $F_n$F n​ denote the $n$n-th Fibonacci number.\n\nFibonacci enumeration conjecture. The sequence $(a_n)$(a n​) satisfies\n\n$$a_0=a_1=1,\\qquad a_2=2,$$a 0​=a 1​=1,a 2​=2, $$a_n=F_{2n-5}-1\\quad\\text{if $n\\geq 3$ is odd},$$a n​=F 2 n−5​−1 if n≥3 is odd, $$a_n=F_{2n-5}\\quad\\text{if $n>3$ is even}.$$a n​=F 2 n−5​if n>3 is even.\n\nThis conjecture proposes a closed form for the enumeration of simple sortable permutations in this two-pop-stack model; the source presents it as a conjecture and gives no evidence of a resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nLapo Cioni, Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith, “Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass”, arXiv:2503.08285 (2025).\n\nenumerative-combinatoricspermutation-patternssorting-algorithms\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimple Permutations Sortable by Two Parallel Pop Stacks with Bypass | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/cioni-ferrari-smith-pop-stack-simple/)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Lapo Cioni, Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith, Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass, arXiv:2503.08285v1, Section 9.2: ... The simple permutations of C, together with one extra non-simple permutation for each odd length, are split by the position of their minimum: position 2, position 3, or later. ... `theorem`; the statement is the conjecture of Section 9.2 of arXiv:2503.08285 and is quantified over every n.\n\n# Simple Permutations Sortable by Two Parallel Pop Stacks with Bypass\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: proved\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/PopStack/PopStackSimple.result10.1016/j.disc.2025.114964Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nLapo Cioni, Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith, Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass, arXiv:2503.08285v1, Section 9.2:\n\n> Let a_n be the number of simple permutations of size n that can be sorted by a machine consisting of two pop stacks in parallel where entries are allowed to bypass the pop stacks. Then a_0 = a_1 = 1, a_2 = 2, a_n = F_{2n−5} − 1 if n ≥ 3 is odd, and a_n = F_{2n−5} if n > 3 is even.\n\nThe sortable permutations are the class C = Av(2341, 25314, 42513, 42531, 45213, 45231, 52314, 642135, 642153); a permutation is simple when it has no interval of length strictly between 1 and n.\n\nMotivation\n\n## Our foothold\n\nThe theorem `D5/S3/Combinatorics/PopStack/PopStackSimple.result` establishes the stated values for every n: the simple permutations of length n in C number 1, 1, 2 for n = 0, 1, 2 and F_{2n−5} − (n mod 2) for n ≥ 3.\n\nGap\n\n## Missing bridges\n\nPre-registration issue 11689 records the literature screen: no later paper, citing record or public repository treats the conjecture. The authors restate it as Conjecture 8 in the Permutation Patterns 2025 booklet, and the abstract of the published version is unchanged from arXiv version 1. This is a bounded negative finding.\n\nRoute\n\n## Proposed approach\n\n  1. Intervals in C behave well under union, contraction and inflation; inserting a new minimum in one of the first three positions is characterized exactly, and a small auxiliary class of two-chain permutations with its simple and prefix-only members is described completely.\n  2. The simple permutations of C, together with one extra non-simple permutation for each odd length, are split by the position of their minimum: position 2, position 3, or later.\n  3. An explicit recursive map T with inverse and an explicit bijection Φ show that the first two parts each have b_{n−1} elements, and a continuation V with proved inverse shows that the third part has b_{n−1} − b_{n−2} elements.\n  4. Hence b_n = 3b_{n−1} − b_{n−2} with b_3 = 1 and b_4 = 2, so b_n = F_{2n−5}, and removing the odd extra permutation gives the stated formula.\n\nFalsifier\n\n## What would falsify this route\n\nThe statement would fail if some simple permutation of C had no place in the three-part split, if T, Φ or V were not bijective, or if the odd extra permutation were simple.\n\nEvidence\n\n## Evidence to collect\n\nEvery structural lemma, the three-part split and both inverse identities of T, Φ and V were checked on all permutations of length at most 11; the counts agree through n = 11.\n\nTriage\n\n## Scope assessment\n\n`theorem`; the statement is the conjecture of Section 9.2 of arXiv:2503.08285 and is quantified over every n.\n\nASSUMED-UNVERIFIED\n\n## Unverified assumptions\n\nThe literature screen is limited to the arXiv, citation-index, OEIS and GitHub searches and the repository checks recorded above; the published journal version was not read in full.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnumeration of simple permutations sortable by two parallel pop stacks with bypass (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/enumeration-simple-permutations-two-parallel-pop-stacks-bypass)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Determine the number of simple permutations of each size that can be sorted by two pop stacks in parallel when entries may bypass both pop stacks, and establish whether the sequence satisfies the stated Fibonacci formulas: a₀ = a₁ = 1, a₂ = 2, aₙ = F₂ₙ₋₅ − 1 for odd n ≥ 3, and aₙ = F₂ₙ₋₅ for even n > 3. ... The paper studies permutation-sorting devices built from pop stacks augmented with a bypass operation. ... — Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass  (2503.08285 - Cioni et al., 11 Mar 2025) in Section 7, “Further work,” subsection “Conjecture on the enumeration of simple permutations sortable by two pop stacks in series”\n\n# Enumeration of simple permutations sortable by two parallel pop stacks with bypass\n\nDetermine the number of simple permutations of each size that can be sorted by two pop stacks in parallel when entries may bypass both pop stacks, and establish whether the sequence satisfies the stated Fibonacci formulas: a₀ = a₁ = 1, a₂ = 2, aₙ = F₂ₙ₋₅ − 1 for odd n ≥ 3, and aₙ = F₂ₙ₋₅ for even n > 3.\n\n### Background\n\nThe paper studies permutation-sorting devices built from pop stacks augmented with a bypass operation. Earlier, it establishes the sortable-permutation class for two pop stacks in parallel with bypass and proves that this class has a rational generating function. The authors then focus on the subclass of simple permutations within that sortable class.\n\nThe proposed enumeration concerns the number aₙ of simple permutations of size n sortable by two parallel pop stacks with bypass. The conjectured values are expressed in terms of Fibonacci numbers and are presented as a possible closed form for the corresponding sequence.\n\n### References\n\nHowever, there is also a potential nice closed form for the simple sortable permutations that we boldly put here as a conjecture: Let $a_n$ be the number of simple permutations of size $n$ that can be sorted by a machine consisting of two pop stacks in parallel where entries are allowed to bypass the pop stacks. Then $a_0 =a_1 =1$, $a_2 =2$, $a_n =F_{2n-5}-1$ if $n\\geq 3$ is odd, and $a_n =F_{2n-5}$ if $n > 3$ is even (where $F_n$ is the $n$-th Fibonacci number).\n\n— Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass  (2503.08285 - Cioni et al., 11 Mar 2025) in Section 7, “Further work,” subsection “Conjecture on the enumeration of simple permutations sortable by two pop stacks in series”--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe reduced-denominator multiplicity conjecture for hard squares on cylinders · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/the-reduced-denominator-multiplicity-conjecture-for-hard-squares-on)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # The reduced-denominator multiplicity conjecture for hard squares on cylinders ... Write each rational function $f_n(t)$f n​(t) after cancelling common factors between its numerator and denominator. ...   * Michal Adamaszek, “Hard squares on cylinders revisited”, arXiv:1202.1655 (2012).\n\n# The reduced-denominator multiplicity conjecture for hard squares on cylinders\n\nmath.CO · posed by 2012 · open\n\n## Statement\n\nLet $P_m$P m​ be the path graph on $m$m vertices and $C_n$C n​ the cycle graph on $n$n vertices. Define\n\n$$f_n(t)=\\sum_{m=0}^{\\infty} Z(P_m\\times C_n)t^m,$$f n​(t)=m=0∑∞​Z(P m​×C n​)t m,\n\nwhere $Z(G)=1-\\chi(\\operatorname{Ind}(G))$Z(G)=1−χ(Ind(G)) is the Witten index associated with the independence complex of $G$G. Write each rational function $f_n(t)$f n​(t) after cancelling common factors between its numerator and denominator. Reduced-denominator multiplicity conjecture. After this reduction, $f_{4n+2}(t)$f 4 n+2​(t) has a denominator with no multiple zeroes, and $f_{4n}(t)$f 4 n​(t) has a denominator whose zeroes have multiplicity at most two. Consequently, for every fixed $n$n, the sequence $a_m=Z(P_m\\times C_{4n+2})$a m​=Z(P m​×C 4 n+2​) is periodic, while the sequence $a_m=Z(P_m\\times C_{4n})$a m​=Z(P m​×C 4 n​) has linear growth. This is a proposed refinement of the established rationality and root-of-unity result for the generating functions; the asserted denominator multiplicity and resulting periodicity or linear-growth conclusions remain open in the source.\n\n## Record\n\nSource\n    \n\n  * Michal Adamaszek, “Hard squares on cylinders revisited”, arXiv:1202.1655 (2012).\n  * MathDB\n\nAdded\n    Oct 8, 2026\n\n## Comments\n\nNothing has been published against this problem yet, and nothing has been said about it.\n\nSign in with an institutional address to take part in the discussion. Reading every thread stays open to everyone.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nco.combinatorics - Panyushev's conjectured duality for root poset antichains - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/331431/panyushevs-conjectured-duality-for-root-poset-antichains)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; In his 2004 paper \"ad-nilpotent ideals of a Borel subalgebra: generators and duality\" (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021869303006380), Panyushev conjectured (Conjecture 6.1) the existence of a certain natural involution on the set of antichains of the root poset $\\Phi^+$ of an irreducible crystallographic root system $\\Phi$. ...   * There are some further developments in arxiv.org/abs/2101.02329 (namely, Type D is addressed as well) but still not a wholly satisfactory resolution.\n\nEmail\n    9. # Panyushev's conjectured duality for root poset antichains\n\nAsked 7 years, 5 months ago\n\nModified 7 years, 5 months ago\n\nViewed 182 times\n\nThis question shows research effort; it is useful and clear\n\nShow activity on this post.\n\nIn his 2004 paper \"ad-nilpotent ideals of a Borel subalgebra: generators and duality\" (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021869303006380), Panyushev conjectured (Conjecture 6.1) the existence of a certain natural involution on the set of antichains of the root poset $\\Phi^+$ of an irreducible crystallographic root system $\\Phi$. The main property desired is that an antichain of cardinality $k$ is sent to an antichain of cardinality $n-k$, where $n$ is the number of simple roots of $\\Phi$. In that paper he constructs the desired involution for Type A, and Type B/C.\n\nQuestion: Have there been any updates/progress on this conjecture?\n\n  * See also mathoverflow.net/questions/83594/… for an MO question regarding further threads of Panyushev's investigation of the root poset\n\n–  Sam Hopkins ♦\n\nCommented May 13, 2019 at 15:21\n\n  * There are some further developments in arxiv.org/abs/2101.02329 (namely, Type D is addressed as well) but still not a wholly satisfactory resolution.\n\n–  Sam Hopkins ♦\n\nCommented Jan 8, 2021 at 15:55--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nco.combinatorics - Panyushev's conjectured duality for root poset antichains - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/331431/panyushevs-conjectured-duality-for-root-poset-antichains?r=31)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; In his 2004 paper \"ad-nilpotent ideals of a Borel subalgebra: generators and duality\" (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021869303006380), Panyushev conjectured (Conjecture 6.1) the existence of a certain natural involution on the set of antichains of the root poset $\\Phi^+$ of an irreducible crystallographic root system $\\Phi$. ...   * There are some further developments in arxiv.org/abs/2101.02329 (namely, Type D is addressed as well) but still not a wholly satisfactory resolution.\n    9. # Panyushev's conjectured duality for root poset antichains\n\nAsk Question\n\nAsked 7 years, 4 months ago\n\nModified 7 years, 4 months ago\n\nViewed 167 times\n\nIn his 2004 paper \"ad-nilpotent ideals of a Borel subalgebra: generators and duality\" (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021869303006380), Panyushev conjectured (Conjecture 6.1) the existence of a certain natural involution on the set of antichains of the root poset $\\Phi^+$ of an irreducible crystallographic root system $\\Phi$. The main property desired is that an antichain of cardinality $k$ is sent to an antichain of cardinality $n-k$, where $n$ is the number of simple roots of $\\Phi$. In that paper he constructs the desired involution for Type A, and Type B/C.\n\nQuestion: Have there been any updates/progress on this conjecture?\n\n  * co.combinatorics\n  * rt.representation-theory\n  * lie-algebras\n  * posets\n  * root-systems\n\nasked May 13, 2019 at 15:06\n\nSam Hopkins♦\n\n26.8k 6 6 gold badges 119 119 silver badges 217 217 bronze badges\n\n  * See also mathoverflow.net/questions/83594/… for an MO question regarding further threads of Panyushev's investigation of the root poset\n\n–  Sam Hopkins ♦\n\nCommented May 13, 2019 at 15:21\n\n  * There are some further developments in arxiv.org/abs/2101.02329 (namely, Type D is addressed as well) but still not a wholly satisfactory resolution.\n\n–  Sam Hopkins ♦--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the existence and number of $(k+1)$-kings in $k$-quasi-transitive digraphs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1206.1897)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Bang-Jensen and Gutin proved that a quasi-transitive digraph $D$D has a 3-king if and only if $D$D has a unique initial strong component and, if $D$D has a 3-king and the unique initial strong component of $D$D has at least three vertices, then $D$D has at least three 3-kings. In this paper we prove the following generalization: A $k$k-quasi-transitive digraph $D$D has a $(k+1)$(k+1)-king if and only if $D$D has a unique initial strong component, and if $D$D has a $(k+1)$(k+1)-king then, either all the vertices of the unique initial strong components are $(k+1)$(k+1)-kings or the number of $(k+1)$(k+1)-kings in $D$D is at least $(k+2)$(k+2). ... @misc{galeanasánchez2012existencenumberk1kingskquasitransitive, title={On the existence and number of $(k+1)$-kings in $k$-quasi-transitive digraphs}, author={Hortensia Galeana-Sánchez and César Hernández-Cruz and Manuel Alejandro Juárez-Camacho}, year={2012}, eprint={1206.1897}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2013.08.007}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1897}, }\nSubmitted 23 Jul 2012\n\n# On the existence and number of $(k+1)$(k+1)-kings in $k$k-quasi-transitive digraphs\n\nHG\n\nHortensia Galeana-Sánchez\n\nCH\n\nCésar Hernández-Cruz\n\nMA\n\nManuel Alejandro Juárez-Camacho\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet $D=(V(D), A(D))$D=(V(D),A(D)) be a digraph and $k \\ge 2$k≥2 an integer. We say that $D$D is $k$k-quasi-transitive if for every directed path $(v_0, v_1,..., v_k)$(v 0​,v 1​,...,v k​) in $D$D, then $(v_0, v_k) \\in A(D)$(v 0​,v k​)∈A(D) or $(v_k, v_0) \\in A(D)$(v k​,v 0​)∈A(D). Clearly, a 2-quasi-transitive digraph is a quasi-transitive digraph in the usual sense. Bang-Jensen and Gutin proved that a quasi-transitive digraph $D$D has a 3-king if and only if $D$D has a unique initial strong component and, if $D$D has a 3-king and the unique initial strong component of $D$D has at least three vertices, then $D$D has at least three 3-kings. In this paper we prove the following generalization: A $k$k-quasi-transitive digraph $D$D has a $(k+1)$(k+1)-king if and only if $D$D has a unique initial strong component, and if $D$D has a $(k+1)$(k+1)-king then, either all the vertices of the unique initial strong components are $(k+1)$(k+1)-kings or the number of $(k+1)$(k+1)-kings in $D$D is at least $(k+2)$(k+2).\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{galeanasánchez2012existencenumberk1kingskquasitransitive, title={On the existence and number of $(k+1)$-kings in $k$-quasi-transitive digraphs}, author={Hortensia Galeana-Sánchez and César Hernández-Cruz and Manuel Alejandro Juárez-Camacho}, year={2012}, eprint={1206.1897}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2013.08.007}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1897}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the existence and number of $(k+1)$-kings in $k$-quasi-transitive digraphs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/replicate/1206.1897)\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{galeanasánchez2012existencenumberk1kingskquasitransitive, title={On the existence and number of $(k+1)$-kings in $k$-quasi-transitive digraphs}, author={Hortensia Galeana-Sánchez and César Hernández-Cruz and Manuel Alejandro Juárez-Camacho}, year={2012}, eprint={1206.1897}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2013.08.007}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1897}, }\n# On the existence and number of $(k+1)$(k+1)-kings in $k$k-quasi-transitive digraphs\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{galeanasánchez2012existencenumberk1kingskquasitransitive, title={On the existence and number of $(k+1)$-kings in $k$-quasi-transitive digraphs}, author={Hortensia Galeana-Sánchez and César Hernández-Cruz and Manuel Alejandro Juárez-Camacho}, year={2012}, eprint={1206.1897}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2013.08.007}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1897}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/replicate/2503.08285)\nciteturn0search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{cioni2025sortingpermutationsusingpop, title={Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass}, author={Lapo Cioni and Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith}, year={2025}, eprint={2503.08285}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.08285}, }\n\n18 views\n\nv1\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{cioni2025sortingpermutationsusingpop, title={Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass}, author={Lapo Cioni and Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith}, year={2025}, eprint={2503.08285}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.08285}, }\n\n## Related papers\n\n# Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/es/abs/2503.08285)\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: last week; Moreover, we describe an algorithm to compute the set of all preimages of a given permutation, thanks to which we characterize permutations having a small number of preimages. ... @misc{cioni2025sortingpermutationsusingpop, title={Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass}, author={Lapo Cioni and Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith}, year={2025}, eprint={2503.08285}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.08285}, }\nSubmitted 11 Mar 2025\n\n# Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass\n\nUniversit degli Studi di Firenze SUNY Brockport\n\nLC\n\nLapo Cioni\n\nLF\n\nLuca Ferrari\n\nRS\n\nRebecca Smith\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe introduce a new sorting device for permutations which makes use of a pop stack augmented with a bypass operation. This results in a sorting machine, which is more powerful than the usual Popstacksort algorithm and seems to have never been investigated previously. In the present paper, we give a characterization of sortable permutations in terms of forbidden patterns and reinterpret the resulting enumerating sequence using a class of restricted Motzkin paths. Moreover, we describe an algorithm to compute the set of all preimages of a given permutation, thanks to which we characterize permutations having a small number of preimages. Finally, we provide a full description of the preimages of principal classes of permutations, and we discuss the device consisting of two pop stacks in parallel, again with a bypass operation.\n\n@misc{cioni2025sortingpermutationsusingpop, title={Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass}, author={Lapo Cioni and Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith}, year={2025}, eprint={2503.08285}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.08285}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Symmetry of Narayana numbers and rowvacuation of root posets (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348321262_Symmetry_of_Narayana_numbers_and_rowvacuation_of_root_posets)\nciteturn0search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2101.02329 ... arXiv:2101.02329v1 [math.CO] 7 Jan 2021 ... Panyushev’s prior work from [18], this proves Theorem 1.4 for Types A, B, and C. ... Panyushev’s nonnesting partition duality conjecture.\n\n# Symmetry of Narayana numbers and rowvacuation of root posets\n\n  * January 2021\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2101.02329\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nColin Defant\n\n  * MIT\n\nSam Hopkins\n\nSam Hopkins\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a Weyl group W of rank r, the W-Catalan number is the number of antichains of the poset of positive roots, and the W-Narayana numbers refine the W-Catalan number by keeping track of the cardinalities of these antichains. The W-Narayana numbers are symmetric, i.e., the number of antichains of cardinality k is the same as the number of cardinality $r-k$. However, this symmetry is far from obvious. Panyushev posed the problem of defining an involution on root poset antichains that exhibits the symmetry of the W-Narayana numbers. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Ferrari_L in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AFerrari_L+in%3Amath)\nciteturn0search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Mar 12 2025 cs.DM math.CO arXiv:2503.08285v1 ... @misc{2503.08285, author = {Lapo Cioni and Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith}, title = {{S}orting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2503.08285}, note = {arXiv:2503.08285v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... Moreover, we describe an algorithm to compute the set of all preimages of a given permutation, thanks to which we characterize permutations having a small number of preimages.\n  * Sorting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass\n\nLapo Cioni, Luca Ferrari, Rebecca Smith\n\nMar 12 2025 cs.DM math.CO arXiv:2503.08285v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2503.08285, author = {Lapo Cioni and Luca Ferrari and Rebecca Smith}, title = {{S}orting permutations using a pop stack with a bypass}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2503.08285}, note = {arXiv:2503.08285v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe introduce a new sorting device for permutations which makes use of a pop stack augmented with a bypass operation. This results in a sorting machine, which is more powerful than the usual Popstacksort algorithm and seems to have never been investigated previously. In the present paper, we give a characterization of sortable permutations in terms of forbidden patterns and reinterpret the resulting enumerating sequence using a class of restricted Motzkin paths. Moreover, we describe an algorithm to compute the set of all preimages of a 